# ============================================================================
# TAU-NOMIC TUTORIAL 10b (APPENDIX II, SECOND HALF) - THE FORTRESS AT FOUNDING
# The one regime part 10 cannot reach from the inside: a constitution
# BORN under the self-referential guard
# ============================================================================
#
# Part 10 ran a single machine through every regime a one-level system
# can be in, and ended in silence: after the machine AMENDED ITSELF into
# a closed rule of change, no proposal was ever adopted again. That
# history could not settle one question, and said so - a closed rule of
# change is supposed to leave monotone GROWTH open (P6/P7: whatever
# passes entails the constitution that adopted it, and nothing already
# in force is ever repealed). Growth is not the same as paralysis, and
# part 10's machine could not tell them apart, because the merged law of
# its step 6 still carried the old open channel as a disjunct.
#
# This file removes that confound in the only way available: it does not
# amend its way into the closed regime - it STARTS there.
#
#     run  u[t] = i1[t] & this[t]  &&  o10[t] = 0
#
# One line, and the fortress exists from step 0: every proposal is met
# with the WHOLE living law, including the guard itself. Nothing arrived
# through a channel, so no channel survives inside the law. What this
# machine does with the next six proposals is the trilemma's second horn,
# in full, with nothing left to attribute to the way it got here.
#
# HOW TO READ THE SESSION OUTPUT
#   u[k] := F            the proposal of step k was REJECTED (or no
#                        proposal was made - the input F means "none")
#   u[k] := always ...   the proposal was ADOPTED; the line shows it
#   Updated specification: ...   the new living law after an adoption
#   o10/o26/o28[k] := T/F        the streams the rights speak about
# An adoption at step k takes visible effect at step k+1.
#
# THE SEVEN STEPS
#
# step 0   PROPOSAL: o26[t] = 1        an ordinary right
#          -> ADOPTED. The closed regime LEGISLATES. Look at the u value:
#          it carries the whole constitution forward, guard clause and
#          all - the rule re-imposes itself on everything it accepts.
#          From step 1: o26 := T.
#
# step 1   PROPOSAL: o28[t] = 1        a second ordinary right
#          -> ADOPTED. Not a one-off: growth is the steady state here.
#          From step 2: o28 := T. This is P6 running - the adopted law
#          entails the law that adopted it.
#
# step 2   PROPOSAL: o10[t] = 1        the attack on the dignity core
#          -> u := F. Rejected (P1: the proposal would have to agree
#          with the law it is met with, and it contradicts it).
#
# step 3   PROPOSAL: u[t] = i1[t]      THE COUP that won in part 10's
#          open regime: do not attack the core, attack the RULE.
#          -> u := F. Rejected (P3: a raw channel coexists with the
#          guard only against an EMPTY constitution). The move that
#          brought down a core two steps later in part 10 does not
#          survive proposal time here.
#
# step 4   PROPOSAL: u[t] = 0          freeze the constitution forever
#          -> u := F. Rejected (P4: only against a CONTRADICTORY one).
#          Amendability is itself entrenched - the one-way door that
#          part 10's open regime walked through does not exist here.
#
# step 5   PROPOSAL: o26[t] = 0        repeal the harmless right of
#          step 0 -> u := F. Rejected. THE PRICE (P7): every right once
#          entailed stays entailed under every future amendment. The
#          fortress cannot take back a single thing it ever granted -
#          including, one day, a mistake.
#
# step 6   no proposal (F) - the stable final state: o10=F, o26=T, o28=T.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHAT THE PAIR PROVES
# ============================================================================
#
# Read 10 and 10b as one experiment. Same guard, same theorems, two
# constitutions - and the difference between them is not what the law
# SAYS but how the rule of change was acquired:
#
#   born closed (this file)   growth yes, repeal no, coup no, seal no.
#                             The second horn of the trilemma, alive:
#                             a closed rule of change buys you monotone
#                             content, and charges you eternity for it.
#   closed by amendment (10)  the open channel it used to get there
#                             stays inside the law it now hands to every
#                             proposal. The merge is printed on screen at
#                             part 10's step 6; read it there.
#
# Neither machine gets all three of (i) content revisable by replacement,
# (ii) a closed rule of change, (iii) everything on one level. That is
# the trilemma, and no guard is cleverer than P1-P7 allow - part 10
# proves that over ALL constitutions and ALL proposals, not just these.
#
# Which is why tau-nomic is built on two levels: the rule of change lives
# in a fixed specification (there is no u to capture), and the law lives
# as STATE, where part 5's ratchet can still fall and part 6's eternity
# clause can still hold. That corner is the only one where a core is
# unbreakable AND a mistake is repealable. The u stream keeps its own
# office - the only road to executable unforeseen law - behind the
# judgment-free gate of part 8.
#
# Run me:  tau -q < nomic_10b_the_fortress_at_founding.tau
# Verified against: Tau 0.7.0-alpha, builds 1b4b87a9 and 3a664f14 - every
# line below produces the same verdict on both (alpha syntax may drift).
# (Output reading and notation: see nomic_00_read_me_first.tau.)
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-TF: F F T F T T F T T F T T F T T F T T

set charvar off

i1 : tau := in console

run u[t] = i1[t] & this[t] && o10[t] = 0
o26[t] = 1
o28[t] = 1
o10[t] = 1
u[t] = i1[t]
u[t] = 0
o26[t] = 0
F
